{"language_code":"eng","language_probability":1.0,"text":"In 1919, a wave of molasses taller than a house tore through Boston faster than a galloping horse. Down by the harbor stood a brand-new steel tank, five stories high, holding two point three million gallons of the stuff. It leaked so badly that the neighbors filled buckets straight from the seams. The company’s fix? They painted the tank brown so the leaks wouldn’t show. Then one January morning, the frozen city suddenly warmed up. The tank groaned, and its rivets started popping like gunshots. Just after lunch, the whole tank split wide open. A brown wave twenty-five feet high swept down the street at thirty-five miles an hour and bent the elevated railway like a paperclip. Luckily, the cold evening air slowed the molasses down. Unluckily, the cold evening air slowed the molasses down. You see, molasses is a lot like honey. Warm from the microwave, it pours like juice. Straight from the fridge, it barely moves at all. So as the sun went down, the whole flood set like glue around everyone still inside it. Twenty-one people died. After years in court, the company had to pay, and America got stricter rules. Big tanks need an engineer to check the math and sign their name on it. For decades, the neighborhood smelled sweet on every hot summer day, and it all happened in January, the one month molasses is famous for being slow.","words":[{"text":"In","start":0.08,"end":0.14,"type":"word","logprob":-2.0265558760002023e-6},{"text":" ","start":0.14,"end":0.18,"type":"spacing","logprob":-0.008617476560175419},{"text":"1919,","start":0.18,"end":1.06,"type":"word","logprob":-0.00005964352728682343},{"text":" ","start":1.06,"end":1.12,"type":"spacing","logprob":-6.437280717364047e-6},{"text":"a","start":1.12,"end":1.18,"type":"word","logprob":-6.437280717364047e-6},{"text":" ","start":1.18,"end":1.2,"type":"spacing","logprob":-1.1920928244535389e-7},{"text":"wave","start":1.2,"end":1.34,"type":"word","logprob":-1.1920928